\description{
A \code{CycleForm} decides what the cycle is extracted from: the level of the
series (the OECD growth cycle) or its growth rate (the growth rate
cycle). The form is applied in \code{\link[=cli_cycles]{cli_cycles()}} after seasonal
adjustment, the transformation and the outlier treatment, and before the
end-of-series extension and the cycle filter, so that growth rates are
computed from seasonally adjusted series and calendar effects do not
enter them (formula F-08 in the package harness).
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{level_form()}: the series itself (default).
\item \code{growth_form(lag, rate)}: growth rate over \code{lag} periods. For a logged
series \eqn{z = \log Y}, \code{rate = "percent"} (default) gives
the growth rate in percent, \eqn{100 (Y_t / Y_{t-k} - 1)}, the formula
BPS-Statistics Indonesia uses for the growth rates of GDP (for example
the year-on-year rate of quarterly GDP, \eqn{k = 4}, or the
quarter-to-quarter rate, \eqn{k = 1}); \code{rate = "log"} gives the log
growth rate \eqn{100 (z_t - z_{t-k})}, lower by
\eqn{100 (r - \log(1 + r)) \approx 50 r^2} points at a growth rate
\eqn{r} written as a fraction (0.12 points at 5 percent). A
series in levels (rates, percentages, indices that were not logged) gives
the difference \eqn{z_t - z_{t-k}} in its own units; so does a
candidate whose \code{unit} in the metadata of \code{\link[=cli_candidates]{cli_candidates()}} is
\code{"percent"}, even when it was logged. \code{lag = NA} (default) means one
year (12 months or 4 quarters), the year-on-year rate; \code{lag = 1} gives
the period-on-period (q-to-q or m-to-m) rate.
}

The rates equal those BPS publishes only when they are computed from the
same data. BPS computes its year-on-year and quarter-to-quarter rates
from series that are not seasonally adjusted, so with
\code{seasonal_adjust = TRUE} in \code{\link[=cli_spec]{cli_spec()}} the quarter-to-quarter rate can
differ from the published one by several points (the year-on-year rate
changes much less). Outliers replaced by \code{\link[=cli_cycles]{cli_cycles()}} also change the
rates around the replaced observations; \code{outlier_threshold = Inf} keeps
the data as published.

The first \code{lag} observations of a growth rate are missing. Without a
\code{filter}, \code{\link[=cli_spec]{cli_spec()}} pairs the growth form with \code{\link[=hp_smoother]{hp_smoother()}}, which
smooths without removing a trend, and the level form with
\code{\link[=hp_double_filter]{hp_double_filter()}}. New forms are added by a subclass of \code{CycleForm}
with methods for \code{apply_form()} and \code{default_filter()}.

The two forms date different turning points (Gyomai and Guidetti 2012,
pp. 9-11). In a growth cycle a turning point marks output moving from
above to below its trend or back; in a growth rate cycle it marks growth
starting to speed up or to slow down.

\strong{Growth rate cycles at the OECD.} The OECD selects and optimises the
components of its CLIs on the growth cycle (deviation from trend); the
CLIs for the growth rate cycle and the classical cycle are only
presentations of a CLI built on the growth cycle (Gyomai, Ahmad and
Astolfi 2017, pp. 276--277). Selecting components on the growth rate
cycle with \code{growth_form()} is therefore an extension of cyclind, not the
OECD method.

\strong{Experimental.} The thresholds of the classifiers were calibrated on
growth cycles (the level form). Growth rate cycles have
more turning points, so the share of extra turning points and the other
criteria have not been calibrated for them; simulation results are in
\code{inst/calibration/forms.csv}.
}
